# Zenodo reads this file when a GitHub release is archived, and uses it to fill
# the DOI record's author metadata. Keeping the name here identical to the name
# used everywhere else is what makes the DOI record resolve to the same person
# as the repository, the package and the personal site.
cff-version: 1.2.0
title: "siemlens: two-stage security log triage with Random Forest and an LLM"
message: "If you use this software, please cite it using these metadata."
type: software
authors:
  # given/family split is fixed deliberately: "uulu" means "son of" and is not a
  # surname, so parsers that guess produce "Akbar Tilek"/"Uulu" and split the
  # author into separate entities across DataCite, OpenAlex and Crossref.
  # Keep this exact split everywhere: ORCID, pyproject.toml, .zenodo.json.
  - given-names: Akhbar
    family-names: Tilek Uulu
    alias: "Тилек Уулу Ахбар (Тилек Ахбар, Tilek Akbar)"
    city: Almaty
    country: KZ
    # Add your ORCID once registered, then uncomment:
    # orcid: "https://orcid.org/0000-0000-0000-0000"
repository-code: "https://github.com/akbar-tilek-uulu/siemlens"
url: "https://akbar-tilek-uulu.github.io/"
abstract: >-
  A reference implementation of two-stage security event triage. A Random
  Forest classifier scores normalised log events on interpretable, leakage-free
  streaming features and decides what a human should look at. Only the events
  that survive that filter reach the second stage, where a language model
  summarises and classifies them under a closed taxonomy and a validated output
  schema. The second stage treats log content as attacker-controlled input:
  fields are allow-listed, sanitised and delimited, the response must satisfy a
  schema, and every entity the model names is checked against the source events
  before it is shown. Includes a deterministic synthetic log generator so the
  whole pipeline runs with no real telemetry and no API key.
keywords:
  - security information and event management
  - security operations centre
  - log analysis
  - threat detection
  - machine learning
  - random forest
  - large language models
  - prompt injection
license: MIT
version: 0.1.0
date-released: "2026-08-17"
